    "id": "pca_cand_53",
    "authorName": "Naunidh Sachdeva",
    "pieceTitle": "Regal and Hot",
    "category": "Poetry / Verse",
    "theme": "Official Nominee",
    "excerpt": "The Wolves and the Tiger By Naunidh Sachdeva One day a pack of wolves appeared, From twisted branches, they prowled and sneered. The tiger moved with steady grace, Unbroken fire upon her face....",
    "fullContent": "The Wolves and the Tiger\nBy Naunidh Sachdeva\n\nOne day a pack of wolves appeared,\nFrom twisted branches, they prowled and sneered.\nThe tiger moved with steady grace,\nUnbroken fire upon her face.\n\nOpportunists who hunt in herds,\nTheir pride is hollow, their venom words.\nChameleons shifting, serpents vile,\nSchemes that flicker, schemes that defile.\n\nBut they knew nothing of her might\nHer silence thundered, her gaze was blight.\nHer wrath was cold, the devil’s flame,\nOne look enough to end their game.\n\nThey mocked her stillness, thought her weak,\nBut strength speaks loud when it need not speak.\nFor tigers reign without display\nA single glance can drive wolves away\n\nThe Wolves and the Tiger\n\nCopy\n\nBy Naunidh Sachdeva\n\nOne day a pack of wolves appeared, From twisted branches, they prowled and sneered. The tiger moved with steady grace, Unbroken fire upon her face.\n\nOpportunists who hunt in herds, Their pride is hollow, their venom words. Chameleons shifting, serpents vile, Schemes that flicker, schemes that defile.\n\nBut they knew nothing of her might Her silence thundered, her gaze was blight. Her wrath was cold, the devil’s flame, One look enough to end their game.\n\nThey mocked her stillness, thought her weak, But strength speaks loud when it need not speak. For tigers reign without display A single glance can drive wolves away\n\nThe Dark Lens\n\nCopy\n\nBy Naunidh Sachdeva\n\nI saw the world through a lens, where everything dark seemed colourful, blessed.\n\nNature and divinity made life conceivable, every dream within reach, every hope believable.\n\nNow I see through a third-eye lens the colours that bloomed are fleeting, black in the end.\n\nA plethora of shades I try to trace, yet nothing changes this empty space. The lens turned dark, closed in its place, no colour allowed to enter its space."
